A||||13||Eric||Breathing Better||||1||
Z||000000||Eric||12-14-2000||09:13 PM||||Thank you to everyone who responded to my 1.6 rocker arm ratio thread.  For those of you who didn't have a chance to look at it, there was an opinion by the majority that the bigger rocker arms wouldn't add enough power to work well on a daily driver.  My major concern was the fact that my car didn't seem to have any guts above 4k rpm.  The engine was worked on about 20k miles ago.  It is a 3.2 with gasket matched manifolds, ported heads, ported exhaust manifolds, and, of course, it has the big ass cam.  While the engine internals have been reworked, the externals such as the intake and exhaust systems are stock.  Is there room for improvement with any of these two components?  BTW, the tuning has been played with too.  I didn't forget to do that part.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-14-2000||09:48 PM||||You'll never get the full breathing potential out of those ported heads until you go with headers. Yeah, I know they're expensive. Ported manifolds will never flow as good as a nice set of headers.<P>Another thing I just thought of. What coil are you running. The old factory coils were bad about giving out at about 4 grand.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||12-15-2000||12:19 AM||||I have a stock 3.4 with gasket matched Fiero intake, larger bore T/B, and the cast iron Camaro manifolds. I "borrowed" the MSD 6a and cannister style Blaster coil from my Suburban and this engine pulls very strong until at least 5500 rpm's. I have heard the same thing that Batboy said about the stock coil/module crapping out above 4000.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||12-15-2000||05:50 PM||||Based on my recent experience with a Jeep, you might check for a clogged catalytic converter.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||12-15-2000||10:09 PM||||Hi Eric, I have been following your posts ever since you were in the process of rebuilding your engine trying to help you figure out what is wrong with your engine. It just baffles me why your engine would have no guts above 4k. I have suggested to you that you may have a clogged exhaust in the past and gave you some simple vacuum tests to follow for that, but that was not it. I have also given you Dyno's from Dyno2000 for your 3.2-liter engine rebuild. <P>Since then I have learned how to utilize the dyno2000 program to get more accurate results especially with the 60-degree engine family. The Dyno's I originally sent you are inaccurate. If you could send me your engine specifications again and all your camshaft specifications I could give you a more accurate dyno simulation. The Dyno's I gave you are way to high for power output. Maybe then I could find out more about the specific characteristics of your camshaft and help you find the problem with your engine.<P>I also have Dragsrtip2000, which I could use to give you a prediction of what your car should run from 0-60 mph and what your car will run in the quarter mile with that camshaft and engine combo. I will need to know the type of transmission you have, axle ratio, tire size, vehicle weight if you have it including you, and the elevation of which you run your car above sea level (it just has to be close not exact).<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||12-16-2000||09:46 PM||||I'm not running a stock coil anymore.  I've substituted the MSD Blaster or whatever it's called.  I don't know if it really helped any, but it's there now.<P>88Formula, I appriciate all the dyno info you made up for me.  It keeps me working at the problem.  Right now I'm messing around with the exhaust y pipe.  The old one was crimped then it cracked, so I gots me a new one that won't fit.  I've had to weld parts from both to make it work, but it still doesn't fit and my car's on blocks.  Once it's running again I'll check it out with the g tech to get some idea of where I am.  I'm not sure, but this could be the problem I've been looking for.  I'll let you know.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||12-16-2000||09:47 PM||||Maybe I should consider a strait pipe for the converter too.||||reg||3
Z||000007||88formula||12-16-2000||10:10 PM||||Well I think I remember you having the GM Performance 12364059 camshaft and if that is true than your engine will make approximately 164 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 203 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. If you have the five speed transmission and your fiero weighs about 2925 lbs. than your fiero will run 0-60 in 6.72 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.87 seconds @ 90.5 mph. <P>I think I remember I quoted you 190 horsepower before, which is way to high.<P>If you change your mind just let me know.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DavidHenshaw||12-17-2000||06:07 PM||||Eric, I have a 4 cylinder Honda cycle that ran great below 3,000 rpm but absolutely would not exceed 3,000 rpm. Discovered one of the exhaust valves had gotten out of adjustment and wasn't allowing normal flow. After readjustment the engine would rev to red line easily. As others have said you may have exhaust restriction somewhere.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||12-18-2000||08:59 AM||||Eric, is there a Dyno near you? Shaun41178 lives in Hamilton, OH.. i don't know where that is.. but there is a Dyno near him.. <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>   take and have your car dyno tested with an exhaust gas analyzer and a wide band O2 sensor. (Competition Data systems) We have a data logger that'll tell you EXACTLY what you need to do.. with out throwing money at it and being disappointed and loosing $. Ported stock exhaust manifolds would be a good start... the headers.. i'd wait till there was nothing left to add/change to do that.. an MSD 6al would aid a bit along with an e-coil Crane PS92 coil. or a matching e-coil from MSD NOT the stock replacement unit for the cars... the following are the dyno number before and after a set of $700+ headers.    <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6headerdyno.jpg">      <P>the 7hp gain in the upper RPM range was NOT worth the 11lbft loss at 2600rpm... or for the torque loss throught the entire band. peak numbers don't mean much at all it's power under the curve that wins the race or plants you in the seat. <P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">      <BR> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 12-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||12-18-2000||11:37 PM||||The exhaust is fixed finally.  I had a chance to run the car a little to see how it felt.  There was no noticable change in power that I could discern.  I didn't put the g tech on it yet, but I would venture to guess there is no change in 0-60 or 1/4mile.  I may try messing around with the timing again just to see.<P>This whole thing has found me taking apart my motor, not in reality, but in my head, to discover the problem.  One little potential gremlin could be the ignition system.  I didn't change spark plugs when I went to the bigger engine.  Could using a plug in a different heat range have any affect especially on such a mild warm over on the engine?  I'm also curious as to what the compression ratio of this engine is.  I've been told anywhere from 9.0:1 to 8.0:1.  I forgot to tell my machinist to check it out last year.  Is there anyone who knows what it would be with Federal Mogul H562CP pistons (slight dish)?<P>The engine already has ported manifolds.  It's kind of weird how the car has all these mods and I still am not able to run sub 16 second quarter mile times.  The only time I ran it was last spring with Shaun at Norwalk.  I managed a 16.4@80 mph as my best of three runs.  NOT GOOD ENOUGH.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rnoswal||12-19-2000||02:55 AM||||Hey Eric, just a thought but maybe your cam timing is off. One tooth is all it takes to make the engine run weak. Too far advanced and no low end, too retarded and no upper end. So unless you have weak valve springs and are getting valve float...well. I am not sure just what controls the timing but maybe it is retarding the timing way too much at higher rpms.  Let us kknow.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||12-19-2000||05:21 PM||||I think you need to get an exhaust gas analyzer on your engine so you can see if maybe your fuel mixture is going lean as the rpm’s and engine load increases.<P>You should also do a compression test to make sure your rings and valves are sealing right. The pressure should be around 120-180 psi.<P>Actually you should perform as many engine diagnostic and performance tests as you possibly can to help you find the problem. <P>Your engine is making about 130 horsepower to get a quarter mile time of only 16.4 @ 81 mph.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||88formula||12-19-2000||06:37 PM||||1, If you put a vacuum gauge on your engine at idle what do you get for a reading? 16-22 inches of vacuum is normal. Around 18-21 is best. <P>2, What happens to the needle when you goose the throttle and watch it decelerate? <P>3,what is your vacuum when you increase the rpm to 2000 and hold it and then to 4000 rpm and hold it? <P>4, What is your vacuum reading if you increase your engine rpm to 5000-5500 rpm and hold it? If you get a monetary low and than a hesitating slow or low return than you have an exhaust restriction. <P>5, is the vacuum reading a steady number or does the needle fluctuate back and forth and how much does it if it does? An intermittent drop or erratic drifting indicates sticky valves.  A rhythmic drop indicates leaky valves. <P>6, does the needle bounce all over the place above 4000 rpm or above 5000 rpm? An increased rate of fluctuations as rpm increases is caused by valve float (weak springs).  <P>7, When you accelerate the engine it should have a monetary low and then return while decelerating to the original vacuum reading or higher. If you get a monetary low with a low return while decelerating than you may have bad compression rings or rings that are not sealing properly.<P>8, How does your engine idle? Does it idle roughly or smoothly? Does it misfire at all? What is your gas mileage? <P>You can perform a running power balance test to isolate the problem to the offending cylinder by running your engine with one fuel injector or one spark plug wire grounded out with the engine running. To do this though you will have to disable the IAC valve by putting the engine in diagnostic mode (grounding out ALDL connector), which causes the IAC valve to close. If you don’t disable the IAC valve the computer will automatically increase the engine rpm. Unplug the IAC valve and take the engine out of diagnostic mode and then start the engine. Note the rpm (should be as low as possible to get the best results (500-600 rpm). If the rpm drops equally for every cylinder you cut out one at a time than each cylinder is performing its share of the work. Make sure you allow the engine to go back to its original rpm before you cut out the next one.  If it does not than that indicates a problem with the low cylinder.<P>There is a ****  load of tests I can have you perform to find the problem. I was a mechanic in the past.<P>||||reg||1
